course: everyday
lesson: 72
title: Keeping garden wildlife safe
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A garden is a home not only for people but for birds, hedgehogs, frogs, mice and insects, and many ordinary things in a garden can be traps for them.
explain: A bucket of water is a pond with steep slippery walls: a small animal that falls in cannot climb out, unless there is a cover or a stick to climb up.
explain: A pile of sticks waiting for a bonfire looks like a perfect hiding place to a hedgehog, so the pile is checked, or better moved, just before it is lit.
explain: Loose garden netting tangles birds and hedgehogs, and slug pellets and rat poison can kill pets and wild animals, so children never touch them.
explain: Animals feel safe with calm people: a boy in Auckland who moves slowly and speaks softly gets to watch a blackbird pull up a worm only a few steps away.
fact: Small animals can fall into a bucket of water and drown because they cannot climb the smooth sides.
fact: A cover on a bucket or a stick leaning inside it lets small animals stay out or climb out.
fact: Hedgehogs like to hide and sleep in piles of sticks and leaves.
fact: A bonfire pile should be checked for hedgehogs just before it is lit, or moved to a new spot.
fact: Loose garden netting can trap birds and hedgehogs, so it is kept tight or taken away.
fact: Slug pellets and rat poison can kill pets and wildlife, not only slugs and rats.
fact: Children never touch poisons, and pets are kept away from them.
fact: Animals feel safe with calm people who move slowly and speak softly.
fact: Sudden movements and loud voices make small animals run or hide.
exercise: Why is a bucket of water left in the garden dangerous for small animals? | They can fall in and drown because they cannot climb out | The water makes them too cold to move | They drink too much of the water at once and then get very ill | The bucket attracts foxes that eat them
exercise: How can you make an open bucket of water safer for animals? | Cover it or put a stick in so animals can climb out | Fill it right up to the top with water so they can float on it | Put it in the sunniest spot | Add some soap to the water
exercise: Why should a bonfire pile be checked before it is lit? | Hedgehogs often hide in piles of sticks | The sticks may be too wet to burn properly | Birds use it to store their food | Checking makes the fire burn hotter
exercise: When is the best time to check a bonfire pile for animals? | Just before it is lit | A week before it is lit | Only after the fire has started | Never, animals smell smoke and leave
exercise: What should be done with loose garden netting? | Keep it tight or remove it | Leave it loose so it is softer | Pile it on the ground in a heap | Put it over the bird feeder
exercise: Why is loose netting a problem? | Birds and hedgehogs get tangled in it | It blows away and makes the garden untidy | It keeps all insects out of the garden | It makes plants grow too slowly
exercise: You find a box of slug pellets in the shed. What should you do? | Not touch it and keep pets away from it | Sprinkle some around to help your parents | Taste one to see what it is | Give a few to the dog as treats
exercise: Why are rat poison and slug pellets dangerous to more than rats and slugs? | Pets and wild animals can eat them and die | They only smell bad to other animals and keep them away | They make the soil too hot | They attract more rats into the garden
exercise: How should you behave if you want to watch small animals? | Move slowly and speak softly | Run up quickly before they escape | Clap your hands so they look at you | Shout so they know you are friendly
exercise: Why do animals come closer to calm people? | They feel safe when nothing sudden happens | Calm people always carry food in their pockets | Animals cannot see people who are still | Animals like to be grabbed gently
exercise: A paddling pool is left full overnight. What could help a frog that falls in? | A plank or stone it can climb out on | A lid put on top with the frog left inside | Adding hot water to warm it | Putting the pool in the shade
exercise: Which garden is safest for hedgehogs? | One with no loose netting and no slug pellets | One with a deep open bucket of water | One with poison under every bush | One with nets lying loose on the grass
test: A girl in Yorkshire is helping build a bonfire for Bonfire Night. What should the family do before lighting it? | Check the pile carefully for hedgehogs, or move it first | Pour petrol on it to make it burn fast | Light it quickly before any animals can move in and make a home | Bang the pile with a spade only once
test: A boy in Dublin leaves a bucket of rainwater by the shed. What is the kindest small change? | Put a stick in it as a ladder or cover it | Move it closer to the bushes so animals can find it | Put a few stones on the bottom | Paint the bucket a bright colour
test: Which is true about slug pellets? | They can poison pets and wild animals | They only harm slugs and nothing else | They are safe for children to hold | They are a kind of food for birds
test: What would probably happen if you ran at a robin to see it closer? | It would fly away and you would see nothing | It would let you hold it | It would hop closer to you to see who you are | It would freeze and not notice you
test: A father puts netting over the strawberries. How should he put it up so it does not harm animals? | Pulled tight so no bird or hedgehog can get tangled | Loose and floppy so it is soft if animals land on it | Lying on the ground around the plants | With big holes torn in it for birds
test: Why might a hedgehog be inside a pile of garden rubbish in November? | It is a sheltered place to rest and sleep | It is looking for the bonfire to keep warm by it | It is there to keep the pile dry | Hedgehogs eat dry sticks
test: Your cat likes to sniff around the shed where rat poison is kept. What is wise? | Keep the cat away and tell an adult the poison is reachable | Let the cat sniff, because cats always know what is poison and what is food | Move the poison into the garden | Hide the poison under the cat's bed
test: Which garden thing is a hidden trap for small animals? | A deep bucket of water with smooth sides | A bird bath with a shallow edge for drinking | A pile of leaves in a quiet corner | A bush with berries
test: left the bucket of water out in the garden overnight, so what | Small animals can fall in and drown, cover it or add a stick | Nothing, animals never go near buckets | It is fine, any animal that falls in can just swim out again | It helps them, they need a drink
test: dad's lighting the bonfire pile we made last week 🔥 | Check it for hedgehogs first, or move it to a new spot | Light it fast before animals come | Just shout really loud first so all the animals run out | Only check it if it is raining
test: can i throw some slug pellets round the veg, mum's out | No, children never touch poisons, they can kill pets too | Yes, they only hurt slugs, nothing else in the garden | Yes, if you throw just a few | Yes, if the dog is asleep
test: wanna see the bird up close so i ran at it n it flew off | Move slowly and speak softly so animals feel safe | Run faster next time to catch it | Shout first so it looks at you | Throw bread at it to make it stay, birds always stop for bread
explain: A hedgehog in a garden in Kent was tangled in a loose football net overnight; it was found weak and cut in the morning and needed weeks at a rescue to recover.
explain: A family in New Zealand left slug pellets on the vegetable bed; their dog ate some and had to be rushed to the vet, very sick.
fact: Loose netting, litter and garden pond edges with no way out trap and kill many hedgehogs, birds and frogs every year.
fact: Slug pellets and garden poisons can poison pets and wild animals that eat them or eat the slugs.
test: footy net still up in the garden overnight, does it matter | Yes, hedgehogs can get stuck and hurt in it, roll it up | No, animals always see nets in the dark and go round them | No, hedgehogs sleep all night | Only matters if it rains
test: dad wants slug pellets on the veg, we got a dog and hedgehogs | Use a safe way instead, pellets can poison pets and wildlife | Pellets are fine, animals hate the smell | Put extra pellets to be sure | Only dogs get sick, never hedgehogs
test: why does a pond need a sloping bit or a plank | So animals that fall in can climb out and not drown | So the water stays warm | So fish can jump out onto the grass to eat bugs | So the pond fills faster when it rains
test: nan's neighbour lit a bonfire and apparently like 20 hedgehogs ran out on fire, what's the real lesson | Check or move a bonfire pile just before it is lit | Hedgehogs love fire, so they hide in bonfires | Never have a garden at all in hedgehog areas | Light bonfires fast so hedgehogs cannot get in
test: someone said if ur dog even sniffs a slug pellet it dies instantly, true?? | Probably exaggerated, but slug pellets can make dogs very sick | True, it dies the very second it sniffs one | False, slug pellets are safe for all pets | True, but only if the dog is a puppy
test: kids are saying the Browns are animal killers cos a hedgehog got stuck in their football net, should i say it too | No, it's unkind; it was an accident, and nets can be fixed | Yes, everyone should know they kill animals | Yes, but only to people who own hedgehogs | Yes, and tell them to get rid of the family and stop being friends
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a bucket of water left in a garden may be hazardous to small animals. | Small animals that fall in cannot ascend the smooth sides | Small animals are poisoned by the water within it | Small animals are frightened by its reflection | Small animals mistake it for food and choke on it
exam: Select the measure most consistent with the established facts for rendering such a bucket safe to small animals. | Covering it or placing a stick inside so they may climb out | Filling it to the brim so that animals can float | Painting it a bright colour so animals can see it | Adding salt so that animals are deterred from it
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises slug pellets and rat poison in respect of other creatures? | They may kill pets and wildlife, not only slugs and rats | They are harmful solely to slugs and rats | They are harmless to any animal larger than a rat | They are harmful only when consumed by birds
exam: Determine the conduct most likely to permit a child to observe garden wildlife at close quarters. | Moving slowly and speaking softly | Running towards the animals with arms raised | Calling loudly so the animals know one is present | Clapping so that the animals remain still
